Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?[w13]=20WIP:=20[DD-13b.W1.3]=20the=20run.sh=20c?=
 =?UTF-8?q?omposed-block=20path=20=E2=80=94=20WRITTEN,=20its=20make=20test?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20still=20owed?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The manager asked for this WRITTEN with its validation separated, which
removes the cost objection my delivery raised. The CONTRACT question it also
raised stands, and I took the option that leaves the shipped contract
untouched: run.sh carries the TARGET's prefix through flush_block's tail,
rather than letting a composed block keep prefix rx — which would need a CLI
allowance [DD-13b.W1.2] deliberately refused, since -p conflicts with --source
by design and cli_extras_clean enforces it.

FOUR SITES ASSUMED THE rx/RX PREFIX and all four are now parameterized:
the pcrec invocation, the driver build (which passed no -DRXT_PREFIX at all),
the NCAPS grep, and tests/lib/size_count.sh's D46 stamp reads.

size_count_row's prefix is an OPTIONAL THIRD ARGUMENT defaulting to RX, so
every caller that existed before this step passes nothing and its rows are
unchanged. VALIDATED THREE WAYS rather than two, because the third is the one
that matters: an rx artifact with no argument reads its stamps as before; a zz
artifact WITH the argument reads the same stamps; and a zz artifact WITHOUT it
reads EMPTY — which proves the parameter is load-bearing rather than
decorative, and that a caller who forgets it gets blank stamps instead of
silently correct ones.

THE COMPOSED PREDICATE IS verify_rxt.py's, DELIBERATELY THE SAME ONE: the file
declares a block name AND this block's pattern carries a by-name reference.
It over-selects in one known way, exactly as leg C's does; keeping the two
identical is worth more than narrowing one, because C1 compares these parsers
and a divergence here would be a divergence there.

THE LOUD FLOOR (K35): a composed block with no target naming it can be built
neither from its own text nor through --source, so it FAILS per case and, when
it has no cases, on its own line. Skipping it quietly would drop its cases
from the denominator and leave the file looking green.

ONE THING I CHOSE NOT TO LEAVE SILENT. H11 builds every target naming a block
and requires each to answer identically to the block's own compile. For a
composed block the first such target IS the block's own artifact, so rebuilding
it would compare an artifact with itself — a tautology wearing a control's
clothes. It is skipped and still COUNTED as built, because it was; the genuine
control for a composed block is a SECOND target naming it, which this loop
still builds and still compares.

verify_rxt.py's skip predicate had a documented population of ZERO. It has one
now — tests/definitions/composed.rxtin is seven blocks that satisfy it — and
the comment says where, and why the corpus total is still 0.

NOT RUN: this needs a full make test behind it and the box belongs to main's
chain. make strict is clean; bash -n and the K37 guard self-check pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01VMRXczPFTZ2zcv2sTzH91X
